he's the hall of famer turner sports  play-by-play announcer marv albert  called last night's wizards celtics  play-in tournament  alongside grant hill you can follow the  eastern conference playing tournament  finale tnt will air  more nba playoff games than any other  network  starting this sunday may 23rd always  great to hear marv albert's voice who  joins us now  marv announcing his retirement following  this year's postseason  mark thanks for joining us why now  always a pleasure why am i joining you  now no why are you retiring  i'm kidding uh i just felt 55 years  of nba and year 60 in broadcasting  overall  it's it's enough and uh my two pugs  trixie and madison  insisted that i spend more time with  them my wife heather  kind of on the fence in that area  but uh i i i think i will miss  a lot of things about broadcasting uh  particularly the nba  it's more uh the preparation and you  know the people that you deal with and  uh and the people in the nba  particularly the coaches who you get to  know  uh so well uh over the years and many  who i've worked with  when they turn to broadcasting but uh  i won't feel it then i think until next  november when the season  starts because i'd normally have the  summer anyway so  uh as i said i just i just think  it was time i don't want to be one of  those uh people that you know just keeps  going and going  i probably could have gone a few more  years but i thought this was the right  time  i was wondering that you were calling  rangers games calling knicks games so it  was territorial there  um and and then when you start to do  national broadcast i didn't know  if any network executive tried to change  you  and your style or voice to have it  conform to i guess what we think should  be a national broadcaster  instead of a local one yeah you know  that  i think that's changed now when you you  know you listen  be it uh the networks uh  over the air or cable people who are  doing games  you have different styles now i think  that's really changed  to the type of voice or the type of  style  uh where everything was kind of straight  too straight laced in terms of  the presentation uh no no one ever tried  uh i must say but that may have  something to do with the fact that  uh the networks were all stationed  primarily in the new york area and they  heard me there  okay so it was acceptable  there and there was a degree of success  there so i  they i never had anybody say anything  like that no  greatest performance you ever witnessed  on the basketball floor  in a game you called  there were a number of them i i can tell  you  in terms of uh an event  the 92 dream team uh which i was  fortunate  to broadcast for nbc that was the  i got the chills when they first came  out in the tournament of america's i  remember that  they had a qualify in portland oregon  and you see the greatest team ever  assembled in sports history you know why  they all weren't walked out  on the court and got the standing  ovation michael and magic and stockton  and barkley and bird and so on  malone um that wasn't just an incredible  moment in terms of major games  several of them involved michael jordan  the the flu game  the six threes against portland when he  wasn't really a three-point shooter but  i recall he came out early  way before the guys came out to warm up  just to shoot threes  and then he hit the sixth and i was  working with the czar  mike fratello uh you recall that name  and also magic johnson and then michael  had that shrug when he hit the sixth  looking over towards our table  he was actually looking at michael  although mike portello claims he was  you know giving him a sign and looking  at him which wasn't  wasn't the case but uh that was  one of the great thrills there were  also situations in my days uh with the  knicks  when they won their first championship  in 6970 there were  several dramatic games when willis reed  got heard  people thought he was not going to play  in game seven  uh and then he walked out out of the  court  late in warm-ups and i recall the laker  team just stopped  and stared will chamberlain couldn't  believe that uh  willis was taking the court and he hit  two jump shots early  yeah and i was i mean that basically  that was his contribution except for the  fact that he inspired them  and it was really uh you know as one of  the great moments in new york  sports when they won because they had  they'd never won a championship then  they won in 73 it wasn't quite  the same feeling they won in la so you  didn't have the new york  fans on hand but uh that was a uh  just a terrific team because of the  style they played with uh you know the  ball movement  uh it's kind of like phil jackson's  triangle in later years  and uh it was such a smart team you know  bill bradley and  uh phil was on that team although he was  injured that year and  uh dave the busher willis clyde  people forgotten game seven because all  the uh  conversation dealt around willis playing  but walt frazier had one of the  all-time greatest playoff games he had  36  points 19 assists seven rebounds  and uh that sometimes is forgotten  except when clyde  mentions it on the air  well he was also going against jerry  west i think he also had six steals in  there  it was andy and he didn't win mvp willis  willis got mvp right he did  yes he had some good games  good games early but he got hurt  actually in game five and  that was red holes who was the head  coach  accepted input from his players  particularly bradley and the busher  and there were no other you know there  were no assistant coaches in those days  but uh bradley and the bushes suggested  a movement offense that really worked  against uh a a laker team that was you  know was chamberlain  west uh it was  uh it was they were a super team  although they only you know jerry west  just won the one championship as it  turned out as great as he was  but uh it was really the  the busher and bradley's input uh  so i i think that had a lot to do with  it because  now you see benches where you know there  are ten coaches you know five sitting  behind  uh the regular bench and it's it's a  completely different situation and  you know it's advanced so uh  uh dramatically in recent years in terms  of what the coaches do and the video  work and the analytics  it's so different than the basics that  red holes motor red hour back  had and of course both had great success  what's the strangest thing you ever  witnessed on the basketball floor  well this was on the floor and off the  floor  that would be the finals game  1994 there was  nix houston and uh it was june  17th i believe and i'm doing the game  uh courtside and all of a sudden  people are looking at the monitors which  were at the press table and it was the  day  of the o.j chase and i'm going back and  forth  with the basketball play-by-play and tom  brokaw back in the nbc studio  and the players are wondering what's  going on  uh in fact uh  pat riley asked what's happening and  uh kenny smith who was then with the  rockets  was right at the uh nick bench it was it  was  uh during a lull in the action and  riley asked him what's going on he said  kenny says to pat  that chasing o.j simpson uh which is not  giving you much information  no but this this went on right  throughout  people uh who were near courtside  when the press was at courtside and they  had also tv monitors  just hunched over all the newspaper men  and watching the chase as this game is  going on  in the finals i would say that's that  was the most bizarre i've been  uh ever been part of the other  uh crazy moments i had when i was doing  the olympics  uh for nbc in 88 in seoul  the shamshill students gymnasium where  the boxing was held  and it was such a crazy setup and it  came out later as we suspected  there were so many fights being fixed  and you know there's all kinds of stuff  going on  but they had two rings going  at the same 